\begin{lemma}[Complete surface shrinkers]
\label{lem:surface-shrinker}
A complete connected nonflat surface with nonnegative curvature
satisfying (B) is compact and has constant Gaussian curvature $\lambda$.
Compactness here needs no curvature bound or noncollapsing hypothesis.
\source{MT2007,ChowKnopf2004}
\dcref{Morgan--Tian, Theorem 9.42 and its compact case, Claim 9.43,
pp. 206--208}
\dcref{Chow--Knopf, Proposition 5.21, pp. 118--119}
\dcref{the surface argument below also supplies compactness under the stated weaker hypotheses}
\lean{PoincareMT.LeviCivitaData.compactSpace_of_surface_shrinker}
\lean{PoincareMT.LeviCivitaData.constantPositiveSectionalCurvature_of_surface_shrinker}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The surface soliton equation gives $dR=R\,df$ and $R+|\nabla f|^2-2\lambda
f=C$. Connectedness and nonflatness imply $Re^{-f}>0$, hence a positive
uniform lower bound for $R$. Myers' theorem gives compactness. The compact
surface rigidity argument, using the soliton identities and Gauss--Bonnet,
forces $f$ constant and $R=2\lambda$.
\end{proof}

